-- Adds 2 more Module 1 lessons, filling gaps found by comparing this academy
-- against Claude Academy's "AI Fluency for Builders: AI Capabilities and
-- Limitations" lesson: this curriculum never explicitly named/defined
-- "generative AI" as its own category (despite it being exactly what FORGE
-- teaches students to build), and never gave students a clear, consolidated,
-- upfront picture of what AI is actually good and bad at.
--
-- IMPORTANT: this migration assumes 20260908050000_new_lessons_neural_
-- networks_rag_agent_eval.sql has ALREADY been applied (Module 1 should
-- already be 7 lessons, ending in "From One Neuron to a Network" before this
-- runs). Run that one first if it hasn't been run yet.
--
-- Same safe negate-then-restore renumbering trick as before. lesson_progress
-- and lesson_quiz_questions key off lesson_id (UUID), never order_index, so
-- this does not touch any existing student's unlock/completion history.
--
-- Final Module 1 layout (9 lessons): What Is AI, Really? / Generative AI:
-- Creating vs. Predicting (NEW) / How Computers "Learn" / A Brief History /
-- Inside a Neural Network / From One Neuron to a Network / What AI Is
-- Actually Good -- and Bad -- At (NEW) / Where AI Shows Up in Your Life /
-- Meet the AI Careers.

-- Pass 1: make room for "Generative AI" right after lesson 1.
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index WHERE order_index >= 2;
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index + 1 WHERE order_index < 0;

-- Pass 2: make room for "What AI Is Actually Good -- and Bad -- At" right
-- after "From One Neuron to a Network" (now sitting at position 6).
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index WHERE order_index >= 7;
UPDATE public.lessons SET order_index = -order_index + 1 WHERE order_index < 0;

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (1, 2, 'Generative AI: Creating vs. Predicting', 'generative-ai-creating-vs-predicting', 'The AI category FORGE actually teaches you to build -- and how it differs from the spam-filter kind of AI.', 10, true)
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"A spam filter tells you “yes” or “no.” ChatGPT writes you a whole new poem that never existed before. Those are two completely different jobs for AI to do.","explanation":"Most of the AI you''ve studied so far in this academy — spam filters, classification, regression — is PREDICTIVE (sometimes called discriminative): given an input, it picks or predicts an answer from a fixed set of possibilities it already knows about. Generative AI does something different: it CREATES brand-new content — text, images, code, even music — that never existed before, by learning the underlying patterns of its training data well enough to produce new, plausible examples in that same style. An LLM writing a new paragraph, an image model painting a picture nobody has ever seen, a chatbot answering with an original sentence — all generative. The key giveaway: predictive AI picks from a small, fixed set of answers (spam or not spam, this class or that class); generative AI produces open-ended new content, with functionally infinite possible outputs.","analogy":"A predictive model is like a bouncer at a club — you''re either on the list or you''re not, one of a small set of outcomes. A generative model is like a chef handed your favorite ingredients — it doesn''t pick from a fixed menu, it creates a brand-new dish that''s never existed before, using what it learned about flavor.","fun_fact":"The “G” in ChatGPT literally stands for “Generative” — GPT is short for Generative Pre-trained Transformer, naming its exact job description right in the acronym.","try_it":"List 3 AI tools you''ve used. For each one, decide: is it PICKING from a fixed set of answers (predictive), or CREATING brand-new content (generative)?","code":"# PREDICTIVE (discriminative): picks from a FIXED, known set of answers\ndef classify_message(message):\n    spam_words = [\"win\", \"free\", \"click now\"]\n    is_spam = any(word in message.lower() for word in spam_words)\n    return \"spam\" if is_spam else \"not spam\"   # always exactly ONE of two answers\n\n# GENERATIVE: builds brand-new text, word by word, that was never written in advance\nimport random\n\nword_options = {\n    \"the\": [\"cat\", \"robot\", \"student\"],\n    \"cat\": [\"sat\", \"learned\", \"explored\"],\n    \"robot\": [\"sat\", \"learned\", \"explored\"],\n    \"student\": [\"sat\", \"learned\", \"explored\"],\n}\n\ndef generate_sentence(start_word, length=4):\n    random.seed(3)\n    sentence = [start_word]\n    current = start_word\n    for _ in range(length - 1):\n        options = word_options.get(current, [\"quietly\"])\n        current = random.choice(options)\n        sentence.append(current)\n    return \" \".join(sentence)\n\nprint(classify_message(\"Click now to win a free prize!\"))  # always one of 2 fixed answers\nprint(generate_sentence(\"the\"))                              # a brand-new sentence, built word by word","visual":{"caption":"Two very different jobs an AI system can do","steps":[{"emoji":"🚦","label":"Predictive AI","caption":"Picks ONE answer from a small, fixed, already-known set."},{"emoji":"🎯","label":"Example","caption":"Spam or not spam. Cat, dog, or bird. Approved or denied."},{"emoji":"🎨","label":"Generative AI","caption":"Creates brand-new content that never existed before."},{"emoji":"✨","label":"Example","caption":"A new paragraph, a new image, a new line of code."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"A model looks at a photo and picks one of 10 fixed labels (''cat'', ''dog'', ''bird''...). Predictive or generative?","options":["Predictive — it is choosing from a small, fixed, already-known set of labels","Generative — it is creating a brand-new photo","Neither — labeling photos is not something AI does","Both equally, with no real difference"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"Picking from a fixed menu of known labels is the definition of predictive (discriminative) AI — generative AI would instead create something new, like generating a brand-new photo."}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What is the key difference between predictive and generative AI?', '["Predictive picks from a fixed set of known answers; generative creates open-ended new content","Predictive is always more accurate than generative","Generative AI cannot process text, only images","There is no real difference between the two"]', 0, 'Predictive AI selects from a small, known set of outcomes. Generative AI produces new content that did not exist before.'),
  (2, 'Which of these is an example of predictive (discriminative) AI?', '["A spam filter that labels an email as spam or not spam","An AI that writes an original short story","An AI that generates a brand-new image from a text prompt","An AI that composes a new piece of music"]', 0, 'A spam filter picks between exactly two fixed outcomes — the definition of predictive AI.'),
  (3, 'What does the "G" in GPT stand for?', '["Generative","General","Guided","Graphical"]', 0, 'GPT stands for Generative Pre-trained Transformer — its name literally describes what kind of AI it is.'),
  (4, 'In the code example, why is generate_sentence() considered generative rather than predictive?', '["It builds new text word by word rather than choosing from a small, fixed set of complete answers","It always returns the exact same sentence every single time","It has no code at all, unlike classify_message()","It only works on images, never text"]', 0, 'It constructs a sequence one piece at a time, capable of producing many different outputs — not selecting from a short fixed list.'),
  (5, 'A model that predicts tomorrow’s temperature as a number is what kind of AI?', '["Predictive — it estimates one specific value from patterns in data, not creating new content","Generative — weather is unpredictable, so any guess counts as creative","Neither predictive nor generative — this is a different category entirely","Both, since it involves numbers"]', 0, 'Even though the exact number varies, it is still estimating a specific value using learned patterns — a predictive task, just a regression one instead of classification.'),
  (6, 'Why can generative AI produce something that looks like it has never existed before?', '["It learned the underlying patterns of its training data well enough to generate new, plausible examples in that style","It has access to a secret database of every possible answer","It randomly guesses with zero relationship to its training data","It copies and pastes from its training data verbatim, every time"]', 0, 'It''s not copying — it''s using learned patterns to construct genuinely new combinations that fit the style it learned.'),
  (7, 'A chatbot answers your question with an original sentence it has never produced in exactly that form before. What kind of AI behavior is this?', '["Generative — new, open-ended content being created","Predictive — choosing from a small fixed set of sentences","Neither — chatbots do not use AI at all","This is impossible for any AI system to do"]', 0, 'Producing new, original phrasing rather than picking from a fixed, pre-written list is exactly generative behavior.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);

WITH new_lesson AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lessons (module_number, order_index, title, slug, summary, coin_cost, is_published)
  VALUES (1, 7, 'What AI Is Actually Good -- and Bad -- At', 'what-ai-is-good-and-bad-at', 'A clear, upfront picture of real AI''s actual strengths and limitations -- so you trust it exactly where you should.', 10, true)
  RETURNING id
), ins_content AS (
  INSERT INTO public.lesson_content (lesson_id, content)
  SELECT id, '{"hook":"The most dangerous thing about AI right now isn''t that it''s too powerful — it''s that people trust it exactly where they shouldn''t, and doubt it exactly where they shouldn''t.","explanation":"Real AI, right now, has clear strengths and clear limitations — and knowing both is what separates someone who uses AI well from someone who gets burned by it. STRENGTHS: versatility (the same model can help with code, writing, and analysis), conversational fluency (a natural back-and-forth), and tool use (calling real functions to check facts, do math, or search — as you saw in Module 6). LIMITATIONS: knowledge cutoffs (it only knows what existed up to its training date, unless given live information), hallucination (it can state something completely false with total confidence, since it has no built-in fact-checker), and unreliable complex reasoning (multi-step logic and edge cases can trip it up in ways that aren''t obvious from how confident the answer sounds). The single best practice: combine AI''s speed and scale with YOUR judgment, especially on anything you can''t easily verify yourself.","analogy":"Think of a brilliant, incredibly fast intern who has read almost everything ever published — but who sometimes confidently makes things up rather than admitting “I don''t know,” and who never actually left their desk to check today''s news. Amazing for a first draft. Dangerous if you skip checking their work.","fun_fact":"One of the most reliable ways to catch an AI''s mistakes: ask about something YOU already know deeply. Errors that would fool a stranger become obvious the instant you''re the expert in the room.","try_it":"Pick a topic you already know really well — a hobby, a subject you''ve studied, something from your own life. Ask an AI assistant a detailed question about it. Where did it genuinely help? Where did it get something subtly wrong or too vague? That gap IS the limitation, made concrete.","visual":{"caption":"Know both sides before you trust it","steps":[{"emoji":"🌐","label":"Strength: Versatility","caption":"The same model helps with code, writing, and analysis."},{"emoji":"💬","label":"Strength: Conversational","caption":"Natural back-and-forth, not a rigid script."},{"emoji":"🛠️","label":"Strength: Tool Use","caption":"Can call real functions to check facts or do math."},{"emoji":"📅","label":"Limit: Knowledge Cutoff","caption":"Only knows what existed up to its training date."},{"emoji":"🎭","label":"Limit: Hallucination","caption":"Can state something false with total confidence."},{"emoji":"🧩","label":"Limit: Complex Reasoning","caption":"Multi-step logic and edge cases can trip it up."}]},"practice":{"prompt":"You ask an AI a question about a very recent event from this week. What should you expect?","options":["It likely does not know about it, since its training has a cutoff date, unless it was given that information directly","It always knows everything happening in real time automatically","It will refuse to answer any question about recent events","It will correctly guess the answer using pure logic"],"correct_index":0,"feedback":"Knowledge cutoffs are real — without live information (like a tool call or search), the model is working from whatever existed up to its training date."}}'::jsonb FROM new_lesson
  RETURNING lesson_id
)
INSERT INTO public.lesson_quiz_questions (lesson_id, order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation)
SELECT ins_content.lesson_id, q.order_index, q.question, q.options::jsonb, q.correct_index, q.explanation
FROM ins_content, (VALUES
  (1, 'What does a model''s ''knowledge cutoff'' actually mean?', '["It only knows information that existed up to its training date, unless given live information","It stops working entirely after a certain date","It can only answer a limited number of questions per day","It refuses to discuss anything related to dates"]', 0, 'Training happens on a fixed snapshot of text — anything after that snapshot simply isn''t in there unless supplied at question time.'),
  (2, 'What is hallucination, in the context of AI?', '["Stating something false with total, confident-sounding certainty","A visual bug where the screen flickers","A model refusing to answer any question","A model that only works with images"]', 0, 'Hallucination specifically means confidently generating something untrue, with no built-in signal that it''s wrong.'),
  (3, 'Why is "it sounds confident" not a reliable way to judge if an AI answer is correct?', '["Hallucinated (false) answers can sound exactly as confident as accurate ones","AI can only sound confident when it is correct","Confidence and tone are randomly generated with no pattern","This is only a problem for very old AI models"]', 0, 'Tone carries no information about accuracy — a confidently wrong answer reads identically to a confidently right one.'),
  (4, 'Which of these is a genuine current STRENGTH of AI, as covered in this lesson?', '["Versatility across many different kinds of tasks","Perfect accuracy on every possible question","Automatic, built-in fact-checking of every claim","Full awareness of events happening in real time, with no tools needed"]', 0, 'Versatility, conversational fluency, and tool use are real strengths — perfect accuracy and automatic fact-checking are not things current AI actually has.'),
  (5, 'What is the single best practice this lesson recommends for using AI well?', '["Combine AI’s speed and scale with your own judgment, especially on anything hard to verify","Trust every answer completely, with no verification","Never use AI for anything important, ever","Only use AI for topics you already know nothing about"]', 0, 'AI is fastest and most useful when paired with real human judgment, not used as an unquestioned oracle.'),
  (6, 'Why does asking an AI about something YOU already know well help reveal its limitations?', '["Errors that would fool a stranger become obvious once you already know the correct answer","AI performs worse specifically on topics experts care about","Experts are not allowed to use AI tools","This technique never actually reveals anything useful"]', 0, 'You can only catch a subtly wrong answer if you already know enough to recognize it''s wrong — that''s exactly why this test works.'),
  (7, 'What kind of task is complex, multi-step reasoning most likely to expose?', '["Unreliable reasoning — a listed limitation, even though the answer may still sound confident","A guaranteed correct answer every single time","A task AI is now provably better at than any human","A task that has nothing to do with AI limitations"]', 0, 'Multi-step logic and edge cases are exactly where current AI is least reliable, even when the phrasing still sounds assured.')
) AS q(order_index, question, options, correct_index, explanation);
